// Crusaders Mainframe — how gate 2.0.0 reads a server's answer, for the life of
// the image (docs/specs/device-api.md §11.2, door-gate.md §14.6).
//
// THE RULES, each one a case in src/contracts/device-protocol/compat/:
//
//   1. One complete object or nothing (BOM and whitespace skipped). A body that
//      is not one adopts nothing and spends nothing.
//   2. Known keys only where they live: top-level keys at the top level, the
//      gate's settings as SCALARS at the top level of `gate` (FC-12: a nested
//      `"lamp":{"unlock_seconds":30}` must never drive the lock).
//   3. Kinds are exact. 12.5 or "30" for an integer setting reads as absent —
//      the previous value is kept and the key is reported in `drift` (FC-11).
//   4. Element grammars only grow at the end. A roster element is read by its
//      first four fields, a day slot by its first two, a key by its first 21
//      characters. A non-empty array of which nothing was readable keeps the
//      previous value and is drift; an explicit [] is committed (FC-1).
//   5. Every 2xx is a success (FC-4).
//   6. `kind` other than "gate" adopts no roster, keys or settings, and is the
//      fault `kind_mismatch` (DG-9). The lifeline — `ota`, `key_next`, `net` —
//      is read from ANY complete object, drifted or mis-kinded, because it is
//      how a board with a broken reader is replaced.
//
// The images below are the gate's own copies of what it adopted. The shared
// roster and key ring keep their contents private, and the board has to put
// them in RTC memory and flash (§14.1), so the gate keeps the canonical form
// and builds the shared structures from it.
//
// No heap, no Arduino headers, valid C++11.

